\begin{abstract}

Industrial agriculture can perturb the planetary nitrogen cycle and enhance atmospheric $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$, motivating ExoFarm as a potential technosignature of sustained large-scale food production. We investigate how stellar irradiation modifies this signal from surface emission to atmospheric abundance and detectability. Four agricultural scenarios, ranging from a pre-agricultural state to an extreme technosphere, were imposed as lower-boundary $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ fluxes in the 1D photochemical model VULCAN for Earth--Sun and TRAPPIST-1e environments. The resulting profiles were propagated into transmission spectra across the JWST NIRSpec PRISM and MIRI LRS ranges and evaluated with PandExo. A complementary Earth-like atmosphere under Proxima Centauri irradiation was used to model thermal-emission spectra and LIFE detectability. Under TRAPPIST-1 irradiation, $\mathrm{N_2O}$ reached larger column abundances and persisted to lower pressures, while $\mathrm{NH_3}$ survived over a substantially broader vertical range than in the Earth--Sun case. In transmission, the strongest ExoFarm residuals occur in $\mathrm{N_2O}$ bands near 2.8, 4.5, and $8.6\,\mu$m and the $\mathrm{NH_3}$ band near $10.7\,\mu$m; even in the highest-forcing agricultural scenario, the most favorable $\mathrm{N_2O}$ feature remains below $S/N=1$ after 100 NIRSpec transits. In thermal emission, LIFE reaches $S/N=4.8$ for $\mathrm{N_2O}$ at 16.5--$17.5\,\mu$m and $S/N=1.44$ for $\mathrm{NH_3}$ at 9.5--$11.5\,\mu$m after 10 h in the extreme agricultural case. A 240 h LIFE retrieval recovers the enhanced $\mathrm{N_2O}$ abundance at approximately $3.1\sigma$, whereas $\mathrm{NH_3}$ remains independently unconstrained. These results identify $\mathrm{N_2O}$ as the more accessible ExoFarm tracer in the configurations explored and show that stellar photochemistry, observing mode, and retrieval degeneracies jointly control detectability.



\end{abstract}

