\section{Nitrogen, Life, and Planetary Perturbation}\label{sec:nitrogen}

Nitrogen is one of the chemical pillars of known life. It is present in amino acids, from which proteins are built, and in the nitrogenous bases of nucleic acids, which allow genetic information to be stored, copied, and transmitted \citep{domagal-goldmanAstrobiologyPrimerV202016}. Its availability therefore conditions the synthesis of essential cellular components and can ultimately limit biological productivity \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018}.

Although nitrogen is essential for life and $N_2$ constitutes approximately 78\% of Earth's atmosphere, this abundant reservoir is not directly available to most organisms. The strong triple bond joining the two nitrogen atoms makes $N_2$ highly stable and difficult to break \citep{domagal-goldmanAstrobiologyPrimerV202016,kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018}. To enter the biosphere, atmospheric nitrogen must first be converted through biological, natural, or technological fixation into reactive forms such as $NH_3$ and $NH_4^+$ \citep{haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}. The biological availability of nitrogen therefore depends on the conversion of the large atmospheric $N_2$ reservoir into chemically accessible forms.

These transformations form part of the nitrogen cycle, a planetary biogeochemical cycle connecting the atmosphere, biosphere, soils, continental waters, oceans, and sediments \citep{gruberEarthsystemPerspectiveGlobal2008,kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018}. The term ``cycle'' should not, however, be interpreted as a single linear sequence or a closed circuit in equilibrium. Rather, nitrogen circulates among reservoirs through a network of interconnected transformations that continuously change its chemical form and oxidation state \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018}. Biological nitrogen fixation provides one of the main entry points of atmospheric $N_2$ into this reactive pool. Its overall reduction can be represented schematically as \(\mathrm{N_2 + 8H^+ + 8e^- \rightarrow 2NH_3 + H_2}\), after which $NH_3$ can protonate to $NH_4^+$ and be incorporated into biomass. Ammonification subsequently returns organic nitrogen to the reduced inorganic reservoir during decomposition, which may be represented schematically as \(\mathrm{organic\mbox{-}N \rightarrow NH_4^+}\). These expressions summarize the net transformations rather than the complete enzymatic pathways involved \citep{domagal-goldmanAstrobiologyPrimerV202016,kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018}.

Once reduced nitrogen enters the inorganic reservoir, oxidation and reduction pathways redistribute it among different chemical forms and reconnect terrestrial and aquatic reservoirs with the atmosphere. Under oxic conditions, nitrification oxidizes ammonium first to nitrite, \(\mathrm{NH_4^+ + \frac{3}{2}O_2 \rightarrow NO_2^- + 2H^+ + H_2O}\), and subsequently to nitrate, \(\mathrm{NO_2^- + \frac{1}{2}O_2 \rightarrow NO_3^-}\). Under oxygen-limited conditions, denitrification proceeds in the opposite redox direction through the sequence \(\mathrm{NO_3^- \rightarrow NO_2^- \rightarrow NO \rightarrow N_2O \rightarrow N_2}\). The sequence need not proceed to completion. In particular, $N_2O$ can escape from the system before its final reduction to $N_2$, providing a direct connection between microbial nitrogen cycling and the atmosphere \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018,schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022,butterbach-bahlNitrousOxideEmissions2013}.

The nitrogen cycle also contains pathways that bypass portions of this sequence. Anaerobic ammonium oxidation (anammox), for example, removes reactive nitrogen by combining ammonium and nitrite, \(\mathrm{NH_4^+ + NO_2^- \rightarrow N_2 + 2H_2O}\), whereas dissimilatory nitrate reduction to ammonium (DNRA) returns oxidized nitrogen to the reduced reservoir through the sequence \(\mathrm{NO_3^- \rightarrow NO_2^- \rightarrow NH_4^+}\). Fixation, ammonification, nitrification, denitrification, anammox, DNRA, assimilation, and other transformations therefore coexist within the nitrogen cycle rather than forming obligatory consecutive stages. Their relative importance depends on the reservoirs and substrates available, local redox conditions, and the microbial communities involved \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018}. This network structure is important for the present problem because an increase in reactive nitrogen at one point in the cycle does not uniquely determine the chemical form or reservoir in which that nitrogen will ultimately accumulate.

Against this natural cycle, industrialized agriculture introduces an additional and technologically controlled source of reactive nitrogen through industrial nitrogen fixation and fertilizer application \citep{gruberEarthsystemPerspectiveGlobal2008,battyeNitrogenNextCarbon2017,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}. The Haber--Bosch process converts atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia according to the net reaction \(\mathrm{N_2 + 3H_2 \rightleftharpoons 2NH_3}\), thereby increasing the amount of biologically accessible nitrogen entering agricultural systems. The added nitrogen is not incorporated completely into crops: part remains in soils, undergoes microbial transformation, or is transported through leaching and runoff, while another fraction is transferred to the atmosphere and hydrosphere as $NH_3$, $NO_x$, and $N_2O$ \citep{battyeNitrogenNextCarbon2017,suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013,tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020}. Agriculture therefore changes both the amount of reactive nitrogen circulating through the nitrogen cycle and the partitioning of its fluxes among planetary reservoirs. When nitrogen inputs exceed crop requirements, $N_2O$ emissions can also increase nonlinearly with the amount applied \citep{shcherbakGlobalMetaanalysisNonlinear2014}. The emergence and development of agriculture as a planetary-scale technological perturbation are considered in greater detail in \autoref{sec:agri}.

These transformations can produce an atmospheric signature because not all reactive nitrogen is assimilated or returned directly to $N_2$. Volatilization transfers $NH_3$ from fertilizers, soils, crops, manure, and other residues to the atmosphere, whereas nitrification and incomplete denitrification can release $N_2O$ and other nitrogen oxides \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018,butterbach-bahlNitrousOxideEmissions2013,battyeNitrogenNextCarbon2017,suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013}. $NH_3$ and $N_2O$ are therefore complementary tracers of agricultural perturbation: the former is closely linked to relatively rapid transfers of reduced nitrogen from the surface to the atmosphere, while the latter is generated through microbial transformations and can preserve the integrated signature of distributed emissions over substantially longer timescales \citep{suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013,butterbach-bahlNitrousOxideEmissions2013,tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020}.

