\subsection{$NH_3$: Production, Emissions, and Sinks}

$NH_3$ has been proposed as a possible atmospheric biosignature because, under suitable planetary conditions, sustaining an appreciable atmospheric abundance requires a continuous source capable of compensating for efficient removal processes \citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a,schwietermanExoplanetBiosignaturesReview2018a,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}. Its high solubility, efficient surface uptake, and photochemical destruction make atmospheric accumulation difficult, so its abundance is governed by the balance between production, atmospheric loss, and exchange with surface reservoirs.

Atmospheric $NH_3$, in equilibrium with its protonated form $NH_4^+$ through \(\mathrm{NH_4^+ \rightleftharpoons NH_3 + H^+}\), can originate from biological, technological, and abiotic processes. Biological sources include $N_2$ fixation, ammonification of organic matter, and DNRA, all of which supply reduced nitrogen to the $NH_3/NH_4^+$ reservoir \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018,huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. Industrial agriculture greatly enhances this reservoir through Haber--Bosch nitrogen fixation and the subsequent use of ammonia-derived fertilizers, while manure and other animal residues provide additional agricultural sources of reduced nitrogen \citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}. Abiotic production is also possible under particular geochemical conditions, including hydrothermal environments, reducing planetary interiors, and proposed photochemical reduction pathways on mineral surfaces \citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a,schwietermanExoplanetBiosignaturesReview2018a}. On present-day Earth, however, atmospheric $NH_3$ is linked predominantly to the biological nitrogen cycle and its technological intensification through agriculture.

Transfer of this reduced nitrogen to the atmosphere occurs primarily through volatilization. The fraction emitted as gaseous $NH_3$ depends on the $NH_4^+/NH_3$ equilibrium and on environmental and management conditions including pH, temperature, moisture, fertilizer type, residue management, and meteorology \citep{suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013,huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. Reactive nitrogen introduced into an agricultural system therefore does not map one-to-one onto an atmospheric $NH_3$ emission flux: part can instead remain in soils or solution, be assimilated biologically, undergo further nitrogen-cycle transformations, or be removed through other pathways \citep{suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013,huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}.

Global inventories place present-day $NH_3$ emissions at approximately $60$ Tg yr$^{-1}$, although estimates vary with the period considered, the included sources, inventory methodology, and whether the reported mass corresponds to $NH_3$ or to the nitrogen contained in the molecule \citep{suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022,huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. \citet{haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022} estimate that anthropogenic activities account for approximately 81\% of the total global $NH_3$ flux and that agriculture alone accounts for approximately 65\% of the global total, principally through fertilizer use and domestic animals. Agriculture therefore represents the dominant component of the present-day anthropogenic perturbation of atmospheric $NH_3$ emissions.

Once emitted, $NH_3$ is removed through both atmospheric chemistry and efficient exchange with the surface. Photolysis and reactions with hydroxyl radicals destroy part of the atmospheric $NH_3$, whereas dissolution, conversion to $NH_4^+$, aerosol formation, and wet and dry deposition return reactive nitrogen to terrestrial and aquatic reservoirs \citep{suttonClimateDependentParadigmAmmonia2013,huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. The ocean constitutes an important sink because dissolved $NH_3$ readily partitions toward $NH_4^+$ and can subsequently be assimilated, transformed through the marine nitrogen cycle, or transferred into longer-lived reservoirs \citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. These sinks strongly decouple surface production from atmospheric accumulation.

The importance of this source--sink balance is illustrated quantitatively by \citet{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. In their exoplanet scenarios, maintaining an atmospheric abundance of approximately 5 ppm requires a surface flux of order $10^{10}$ molecules cm$^{-2}$ s$^{-1}$ when surface sinks are saturated, whereas the required flux rises to approximately $10^{15}$ molecules cm$^{-2}$ s$^{-1}$ when deposition and surface removal remain efficient. These values are not predictions for terrestrial agriculture, but the five-order-of-magnitude contrast demonstrates that a large production flux alone does not determine atmospheric $NH_3$ abundance: the efficiency of surface and atmospheric sinks can dominate the resulting atmospheric state.

\subsection{$N_2O$: Sources, Biosignature, and Stratospheric Sink}

$N_2O$ has been proposed as a promising atmospheric biosignature because most of its present-day terrestrial production is associated with microbial nitrogen metabolism, whereas known abiotic sources are comparatively limited and generally require specific planetary conditions \citep{seguraBiosignaturesEarthLikePlanets2005,schwietermanExoplanetBiosignaturesReview2018a}. Its near- and mid-infrared absorption bands have consequently been investigated in models of Earth through time and of terrestrial exoplanet spectra, although several bands overlap with absorbers such as $H_2O$, $CO_2$, and $CH_4$ \citep{kalteneggerSpectralEvolutionEarthlike2007,kalteneggerHighresolutionTransmissionSpectra2020,meadowsFeasibilityDetectingBiosignatures2023}. For the ExoFarm problem, however, an equally important property is that the atmospheric abundance of $N_2O$ is not controlled by its surface flux alone: stellar ultraviolet irradiation regulates its principal photochemical losses, allowing comparable surface production rates to generate different atmospheric concentrations around different stellar types \citep{seguraBiosignaturesEarthLikePlanets2005,schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022}.

On Earth, $N_2O$ production combines relatively small abiotic contributions with much larger biological and anthropogenically enhanced microbial sources. Proposed abiotic pathways include production associated with electrical discharges and other atmospheric reactions, as well as chemiodenitrification of oxidized nitrogen species in the presence of reductants such as $Fe^{2+}$; these contributions are generally small compared with the biological budget \citep{tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024,grenfellPotentialBiosignaturesSuperEarth2013,schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022}. Most $N_2O$ is produced in soils, inland waters, and oceans through microbial nitrogen transformations. Nitrification can release $N_2O$ as a by-product, whereas denitrification produces it as an intermediate when nitrate and nitrite are not completely reduced to $N_2$ \citep{butterbach-bahlNitrousOxideEmissions2013,schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022,tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020}. The balance between $N_2O$ production and its complete reduction to $N_2$ depends on oxygen availability, moisture, temperature, pH, carbon availability, and microbial-community structure \citep{kuypersMicrobialNitrogencyclingNetwork2018,butterbach-bahlNitrousOxideEmissions2013}. Biogeochemical models further indicate that environmental, metabolic, or cofactor limitations capable of preventing complete denitrification could support substantially larger biological $N_2O$ fluxes than those observed on modern Earth \citep{schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022,schwietermanOverviewExoplanetBiosignatures2024}.

Human activities amplify these microbial pathways primarily by increasing the supply and redistribution of reactive nitrogen. Fertilizers and manure increase nitrogen availability in soils, while volatilization, leaching, runoff, and subsequent deposition can transport anthropogenic nitrogen into other terrestrial and aquatic environments where further $N_2O$ production occurs \citep{tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020,tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024}. Additional anthropogenic sources include fossil-fuel combustion, chemical production, wastewater treatment, and biomass burning \citep{tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020,tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024}.

The global $N_2O$ source is approximately 17--18 Tg N yr$^{-1}$ according to recent global syntheses \citep{tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020,tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024}. Natural sources, dominated by soils and the ocean and including additional aquatic and coastal reservoirs in recent inventories, contribute approximately 55--65\% of the total, whereas anthropogenic sources account for roughly 35--45\%, with differences among inventories and periods considered \citep{haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022,tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020,tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024}. Within this anthropogenic perturbation, agriculture is the dominant contributor. Direct and indirect agricultural emissions associated with fertilizer and manure use, animal production, and the subsequent redistribution of reactive nitrogen represent approximately 26--30\% of the total global $N_2O$ source, corresponding to roughly 70--74\% of anthropogenic emissions \citep{tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020,tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024}. Thus, natural nitrogen cycling remains responsible for most global $N_2O$ production, while agriculture dominates the quantified anthropogenic enhancement.

Once emitted, $N_2O$ behaves very differently from $NH_3$. It is relatively stable in the troposphere and is transported into the stratosphere, where ultraviolet photolysis through \(\mathrm{N_2O + h\nu \rightarrow N_2 + O(^1D)}\) constitutes its principal sink, while reaction with electronically excited oxygen provides a secondary loss channel, including \(\mathrm{N_2O + O(^1D) \rightarrow 2NO}\). The latter pathway couples $N_2O$ destruction to stratospheric ozone chemistry through the production of $NO$ \citep{pratherMeasuringNitrousOxideLifetime2015,schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022}. Because $N_2O$ is not efficiently removed by rapid surface deposition, its atmospheric lifetime on present-day Earth is approximately 116--120 years, much longer than that of $NH_3$ \citep{tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020,pratherMeasuringNitrousOxideLifetime2015}. It can therefore integrate geographically distributed nitrogen-cycle emissions over long spatial and temporal scales. Its atmospheric abundance nevertheless remains sensitive to transport and photochemical loss rates, which vary with atmospheric structure and with the ultraviolet spectrum of the host star \citep{schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022,grenfellPotentialBiosignaturesSuperEarth2013}.

\bigskip

The contrasting behavior of $NH_3$ and $N_2O$ summarizes why perturbing the nitrogen cycle does not translate into a unique atmospheric response. Industrial nitrogen fixation and fertilizer application increase the amount of reactive nitrogen entering the planetary nitrogen cycle, but only a fraction of that perturbation reaches the atmosphere as either molecule. $NH_3$ is strongly controlled by volatilization, aqueous partitioning, deposition, and other rapid surface exchanges, whereas $N_2O$ is produced largely through microbial transformations and, once emitted, persists long enough to integrate distributed sources over much broader spatial and temporal scales. Consequently, an agricultural nitrogen input cannot be converted into atmospheric $NH_3$ and $N_2O$ abundances through a simple proportional scaling. A physically consistent assessment must instead begin with surface emission fluxes and propagate them through the transport and molecule-specific chemical sinks that determine the resulting vertical atmospheric profiles. Because several of these sinks are photochemical and depend on the stellar radiation field, the same agricultural regime can produce different $NH_3$ and $N_2O$ atmospheric signatures around different host stars. This source--cycle--atmosphere connection provides the physical basis for the flux-driven photochemical treatment adopted in this work.

\section{Agriculture as a Planetary Technological Transition}\label{sec:agri}

Agriculture designates a set of practices through which societies deliberately manage food production through cultivation, animal management, and landscape transformation. Compared with foraging, which extracts distributed wild resources, it concentrates part of primary production in selected species and spaces \citep{bowlesChoiNeolithicAgriculturalRevolution2019,weisdorfFromForagingToFarming2005}. In biogeochemical terms, this management reorganizes the flows of biomass, water, and nutrients; when it intensifies and extends over large areas, its effects may cease to be confined to fields and acquire a planetary expression \citep{haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022,franckHarvestingSunNew2011}.

The transition from foraging to food production reorganized labor, settlement, and resource management, even though early agriculture did not necessarily improve labor productivity. Once established, agricultural systems made it possible to concentrate, store, and redistribute food and supported broader changes in settlement, specialization, and technology \citep{weisdorfFromForagingToFarming2005,bowlesChoiNeolithicAgriculturalRevolution2019}.

The evolution of agricultural nutrient management reached a new scale with industrial nitrogen fixation. Before it, agricultural fertility depended mainly on the recycling of waste and manure, the incorporation of legumes, and limited external sources such as guano and nitrate deposits \citep{jenkinsonImpactHumansNitrogenCycle2001,gallowayChronologyHumanUnderstandingNitrogen2013}. The Haber--Bosch process transformed this constraint by converting atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia at industrial scales, expanding the supply of reactive nitrogen available for fertilizer production \citep{gallowayChronologyHumanUnderstandingNitrogen2013,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}. By relaxing a major constraint on agricultural nitrogen supply, this technological transition increased the food-production capacity of agricultural systems and contributed to the expansion of the population that intensive agriculture could sustain. Between 1961 and 2007, global nitrogen inputs increased by 134\%, while yields increased by 120\%; during the same period, mineral fertilizer doubled its relative share, whereas the contributions of manure and biological fixation decreased in aggregate \citep{conantPatternsNitrogenUseRecovery2013}.

This intensification did not mean that all added nitrogen was incorporated into the harvest: approximately 40\% of the external nitrogen inputs considered was recovered in harvested products \citep{conantPatternsNitrogenUseRecovery2013}. The remaining fraction may stay in the soil or be redistributed to continental waters and the atmosphere through leaching, runoff, and gaseous emissions; these losses promote eutrophication and connect agriculture with atmospheric chemistry, water quality, and climate \citep{jenkinsonImpactHumansNitrogenCycle2001,zhangGlobalNitrogenCycle2020,gongAnthropogenicDrivenPerturbationsNitrogen2024,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}. Industrial agriculture thus connects food production with processes that operate simultaneously in soils, continental waters, and the atmosphere.

In the context of ExoFarm, the scale of this perturbation can be related to population, food production, land use, productivity, and technological organization. Together, these factors determine agricultural demand for biomass and nutrients and, consequently, the magnitude of the nitrogen inputs and losses that can propagate through the planetary nitrogen cycle.

Human carrying capacity is commonly used to estimate the population that can be sustained under a given set of resource, productive, technological, and environmental conditions \citep{cohenPopulationGrowthEarths1995,franckHarvestingSunNew2011}. It therefore provides a useful way to establish orders of magnitude for the scale of agricultural activity that different planetary systems might sustain. Carrying capacity depends on assumptions about productivity, land availability, diet, resource use, technology, and social organization. Industrial nitrogen fixation provides a historical example of how technological change can shift a previously limiting constraint on food production. Carrying-capacity estimates therefore provide scenario-dependent bounds on the population and agricultural activity that a planetary system might sustain.

Published estimates of human carrying capacity span several orders of magnitude, reflecting substantial differences in the assumptions and constraints adopted by each model. \citet{cohenPopulationGrowthEarths1995} compiled 65 historical estimates, with median lower and upper bounds of 7.7 and 12 billion people, respectively, although the full set ranged from fewer than 1 billion to more than 1 trillion. Models formulated more explicitly around agricultural production remain similarly sensitive to their assumptions. \citet{mullanPopulationGrowthEnergy2019} estimated carrying capacities of 17--180 billion under contemporary yields and 40--400 billion when historical productivity trends were extrapolated, whereas the spatially explicit analysis of \citet{franckHarvestingSunNew2011} produced values between 11.4 and 281.5 billion depending on land use and the degree to which calorie production was maximized. The breadth of these estimates illustrates how strongly carrying capacity depends on assumptions about productivity, technology, resource use, land allocation, and the other constraints embedded in each model.

For ExoFarm, these projections provide orders of magnitude for exploring how different regimes of population, land use, productivity, diet, and technology modify agricultural nitrogen demand and its associated losses. Their range also reflects the uncertainty inherent in projecting long-term agricultural futures when technological change can alter the constraints embedded in present-day models. These considerations motivate using the technosignature framework and the ExoFarm and technosphere scenarios developed by \citet{haqq-misraSearchingTechnosignaturesExoplanetary2022,haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022,haqq-misraProjectionsEarthsTechnosphere2025} as references for the agricultural forcing levels explored in the following sections. The specific surface-flux scaling adopted for these scenarios is described in \autoref{sec:photo}.

