\section{ExoFarm Scenarios Across Stellar Environments}
\label{sec:photo}

To model atmospheric responses across increasing agricultural nitrogen forcing,
we defined four ExoFarm scenarios based on the lower-boundary fluxes of
$\mathrm{NH_3}$ and $\mathrm{N_2O}$: a pre-agricultural reference state (A0),
a present-day terrestrial baseline (A1), a moderate ExoFarm perturbation (A2),
and an extreme regime (A3).

Atmospheric compositions were computed using the one-dimensional kinetic
photochemical model VULCAN
\citep{tsaiVULCANOpensourceValidated2017,
tsaiComparativeStudyAtmospheric2021}. VULCAN has been validated for oxidizing
atmospheres through simulations of modern Earth and allows surface emissions
and deposition to be imposed as lower-boundary conditions. For each planetary
environment, the pressure--temperature profile and eddy-diffusion coefficient
($K_{zz}$) were held fixed while the chemical network evolved toward steady
state. The ExoFarm sequence thus isolates the effect of surface chemical
forcing within a given atmospheric background state.

For species $i\in\{\mathrm{NH_3},\mathrm{N_2O}\}$, the surface flux was
defined as

\begin{equation}
F_i(A_j)
=
F_i(A_0)
+
\alpha_{i,j}
\left[
F_i(A_1)-F_i(A_0)
\right],
\end{equation}

where $\alpha_{i,j}$ governs the magnitude of the perturbation relative to
the A0--A1 terrestrial increment.

Scenario A0 represents natural background emissions. Following
\citet{seinfeldAtmosphericChemistryPhysics2016}, who attribute approximately
19\% of global $\mathrm{NH_3}$ emissions to natural processes and 65\% to
agriculture, we set A0 to the natural component and A1 to the combined
natural-plus-agricultural flux, yielding
$2.94\times10^9$ and
$1.30\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$, respectively.

For $\mathrm{N_2O}$, A0 was derived from the natural emission budget of
approximately $11.8\,\mathrm{Tg\,N\,yr^{-1}}$ estimated by
\citet{tianGlobalNitrousOxide2024}, giving
$F_{\mathrm{N_2O}}(A_0)=1.58\times10^9\,
\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$.
For A1, we adopted
$F_{\mathrm{N_2O}}(A_1)=2.30\times10^9\,
\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$,
consistent with \citet{seinfeldAtmosphericChemistryPhysics2016} and the
modern-Earth VULCAN validation of
\citet{tsaiComparativeStudyAtmospheric2021}. The A0--A1 interval for
$\mathrm{N_2O}$ was therefore treated as an effective present-day
anthropogenic enhancement for the subsequent scaling.

The enhanced scenarios scaled this A0--A1 increment. For A2, we adopted the
moderate ExoFarm parameters of
\citet{haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022}, corresponding to
$\alpha_{\mathrm{NH_3},A2}=3.50$ and
$\alpha_{\mathrm{N_2O},A2}=2.55$.
Scenario A3 adopted $\alpha=15$ for both species, following the
upper-envelope technosphere case (S2) modeled by
\citet{haqq-misraProjectionsEarthsTechnosphere2025}.

Within the population--food-production framework discussed in \autoref{sec:agri}, these enhanced cases represent agricultural futures in which larger populations and food requirements increase the scale and intensity of food production. Where this expansion is sustained by industrial nitrogen fixation, greater inputs of nitrogen fertilizer can amplify reactive nitrogen losses to the atmosphere as $\mathrm{NH_3}$ and $\mathrm{N_2O}$. These published contrasts were therefore used to define the relative spacing of the surface-forcing scenarios, while the resulting atmospheric abundances were calculated by VULCAN.

Table~\ref{tab:scenarios} lists the resulting lower-boundary fluxes.
Apart from the prescribed $\mathrm{NH_3}$ and $\mathrm{N_2O}$ surface
fluxes, the remaining lower-boundary chemical conditions were retained from
the present-day Earth VULCAN configuration of \citet{tsaiComparativeStudyAtmospheric2021} and held fixed across A0--A3.
Among these retained boundary conditions, the deposition velocities for
$\mathrm{NH_3}$ and $\mathrm{N_2O}$ were $1\,\mathrm{cm\,s^{-1}}$ and $10^{-4}\,\mathrm{cm\,s^{-1}}$, respectively. No direct agricultural enhancement of $\mathrm{CH_4}$ was imposed, so any
methane variation across the sequence arose from the response of the
photochemical network to the nitrogen forcing.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Lower-boundary fluxes adopted for the ExoFarm scenarios.
Flux values correspond to the numerical inputs used in the VULCAN
calculations.}
\label{tab:scenarios}
\begin{tabular}{l l c c c c}
\hline\hline
ID & Scenario &
$\alpha_{\mathrm{NH_3}}$ &
$\alpha_{\mathrm{N_2O}}$ &
$\mathrm{NH_3}$ flux &
$\mathrm{N_2O}$ flux \\
& & & &
\multicolumn{2}{c}{($\mathrm{molecules\,cm^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$)} \\
\hline
A0 & Pre-agricultural
& 0 & 0
& $2.94\times10^9$
& $1.58\times10^9$ \\

A1 & Modern Earth
& 1 & 1
& $1.30\times10^{10}$
& $2.30\times10^9$ \\

A2 & Moderate ExoFarm
& 3.50 & 2.55
& $3.82\times10^{10}$
& $3.416\times10^9$ \\

A3 & Extreme ExoFarm
& 15 & 15
& $1.54\times10^{11}$
& $1.238\times10^{10}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We considered three stellar irradiation environments. For the Sun, we used
the reference solar spectrum of \citet{GUEYMARD2018434}. For Proxima
Centauri, we used the MUSCLES v22 spectral energy distribution (SED) for
GJ~551 distributed through the Mikulski Archive for Space Telescopes (MAST) \citep{franceMUSCLESTREASURYSURVEY2016,
youngbloodMUSCLESTREASURYSURVEY2016,loydMUSCLESTREASURYSURVEY2016}. For TRAPPIST-1, we adopted the Mega-MUSCLES SED presented by \citet{wilsonMegaMUSCLESSpectralEnergy2021}. Figure~\ref{fig:star} compares the corresponding stellar-surface fluxes. Their spectral energy distributions differ strongly in the ultraviolet, where molecular photolysis and radical production strongly influence trace-gas lifetimes.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{imgs/stellar_spectra_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Stellar surface-flux spectra adopted for the Sun (G2V),
TRAPPIST-1 (M8V), and Proxima Centauri (M5.5V). Wavelengths below
$400~\mathrm{nm}$ are shaded to highlight the region where differences in
stellar irradiation are particularly relevant for atmospheric
photochemistry. Proxima Centauri is included as the stellar environment used
in the subsequent thermal-emission and LIFE calculations.}
\label{fig:star}
\end{figure}


The vertical photochemical comparison focused on Earth--Sun and TRAPPIST-1e. The terrestrial baseline adopted a 1 bar reference atmosphere with $X_{\mathrm{CO_2}} = 4\times10^{-4}$. For TRAPPIST-1e, we adopted the
1 bar modern-Earth-like pressure--temperature profile of
\citet{linDifferentiatingModernPrebiotic2021}, with
$X_{\mathrm{CO_2}} = 0.036$. In that scenario, the enhanced $\mathrm{CO_2}$ abundance maintains a mean surface temperature above the freezing point of water, making the profile compatible with surface
liquid-water conditions. For vertical transport, we retained the present-day Earth $K_{zz}$ profile used in the VULCAN validation of \citet{tsaiComparativeStudyAtmospheric2021}, interpolating it onto the TRAPPIST-1e pressure grid.

The calculations employed the full S--N--C--H--O photochemical network implemented in VULCAN \citep{tsaiComparativeStudyAtmospheric2021}, comprising
100 chemical species and 1284 reactions. Each atmospheric column was discretized into 120 vertical layers extending from approximately 1 bar to $5\times10^{-8}$ bar. VULCAN evolved the chemical abundances through chemical production and loss, photolysis, vertical transport, surface emissions, and deposition toward steady state under the prescribed atmospheric structure.

Figure~\ref{fig:chem} shows the resulting vertical abundance profiles. To summarize the integrated atmospheric response, we calculated the column-averaged mixing ratio, 

$$
\overline{x}_{i,\mathrm{col}} = \frac{1}
{p_{\mathrm{bot}}-p_{\mathrm{top}}} \int_{p_{\mathrm{top}}}^{p_{\mathrm{bot}}} x_i(p)\,dp,
$$

where $x_i(p)$ is the local volume mixing ratio of species $i$. This quantity
represents an average abundance over the atmospheric pressure column.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{imgs/photochemical_summary_grid.pdf}
\caption{Photochemical response of the ExoFarm A0--A3 sequence. The first two columns show the vertical mixing-ratio profiles calculated with VULCAN for the Earth--Sun and TRAPPIST-1e configurations, respectively. Scenario
colors are A0 pre-agricultural (light green), A1 modern Earth (cyan), A2 moderate ExoFarm (purple), and A3 extreme ExoFarm (magenta). The third column shows the corresponding column-averaged mixing ratios, with
Earth--Sun represented by brown circles and solid lines and TRAPPIST-1e by violet triangles and dashed lines. The dash-dotted horizontal line marks the Earth--Sun A1 reference for each species, and the annotations indicate the ratio relative to this baseline. Rows correspond, from top to bottom, to $\mathrm{N_2O}$, $\mathrm{NH_3}$, $\mathrm{O_3}$, $\mathrm{CH_4}$, and OH.} \label{fig:chem}
\end{figure*}

For $\mathrm{N_2O}$, the atmospheric response closely followed the imposed surface forcing. Because the scenario coefficients scaled the A0--A1 increment, the resulting total surface fluxes corresponded to approximately $0.69$, $1$, $1.49$, and $5.38$ times the A1 value across A0--A3. The Earth--Sun column averages followed nearly the same progression, reaching
$0.69$, $1$, $1.5$, and $5.1$ times the Earth--Sun A1 reference. A similar relative response was obtained for TRAPPIST-1e: when normalized to its own A1 value, the A2 and A3 column averages increased by approximately
$1.5$ and $5.36$, respectively. The atmospheric $\mathrm{N_2O}$ response therefore remained close to proportional with the imposed surface forcing
in both environments.

The stellar environment mainly affected the abundance reached for a given forcing and the vertical extent of the $\mathrm{N_2O}$ profile. At A3, the TRAPPIST-1e column average reached $7.5$ times the Earth--Sun A1 reference, compared with $5.1$ times for Earth--Sun. Elevated $\mathrm{N_2O}$ mixing ratios also remained abundant to lower pressures under TRAPPIST-1 irradiation.

$\mathrm{NH_3}$ showed a stronger dependence on atmospheric processing. The imposed A3 surface flux corresponded to approximately $11.85$ times its A1
value, while the Earth--Sun column average increased to $5.9$ times the A1 reference. Under TRAPPIST-1e conditions, the A3 column average reached $22$ times the Earth--Sun A1 reference and approximately $12.2$ times the
TRAPPIST-1e A1 value.

The vertical profiles show the same environmental dependence. Under solar irradiation, $\mathrm{NH_3}$ declined rapidly above the lower atmosphere and the separation among A0--A3 diminished with altitude. Under TRAPPIST-1 irradiation, the scenario separation persisted to substantially lower pressures, preserving the imposed surface enhancement through a larger fraction of the atmospheric column. The TRAPPIST-1e atmosphere therefore
maintained higher $\mathrm{NH_3}$ abundances over a broader vertical region.

To examine the contribution of the stellar spectrum, we performed an additional controlled calculation retaining a terrestrial pressure--temperature profile, terrestrial $K_{zz}$, and modern-Earth composition under the TRAPPIST-1 stellar spectrum. This calculation reproduced the same qualitative increase in the vertical persistence of
$\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$, supporting a substantial contribution from the stellar radiation field to the behavior obtained in the TRAPPIST-1e branch.

The behavior of $\mathrm{O_3}$ and $\mathrm{CH_4}$ reflects the broader response of atmospheric chemistry to the stellar environment. Across the TRAPPIST-1e sequence, both species changed little from A0 to A3 despite their large displacement from the Earth--Sun abundances. The
column-averaged $\mathrm{O_3}$ abundance remained near
$1.7\times10^{-3}$ of the Earth--Sun A1 reference, while
$\mathrm{CH_4}$ remained near approximately $56$ times the same reference.

The low $\mathrm{O_3}$ abundance obtained for TRAPPIST-1e is consistent
with photochemical calculations of Earth-like atmospheres around cool
M dwarfs, where reduced irradiation in the relevant
$\mathrm{O_2}$-photolysis bands can weaken Chapman ozone production and
substantially alter the ozone profile
\citep{grenfellPotentialBiosignaturesSuperEarth2013,
wunderlichDetectabilityAtmosphericFeatures2019}. The enhanced
$\mathrm{CH_4}$ abundance is likewise consistent with the longer methane
lifetimes obtained in photochemical models of Earth-like M-dwarf planets,
where the stellar UV spectrum modifies ozone photolysis, radical production,
and methane oxidation
\citep{seguraBiosignaturesEarthLikePlanets2005,
grenfellPotentialBiosignaturesSuperEarth2013,
wunderlichDetectabilityAtmosphericFeatures2019}.

The Earth--Sun sequence showed a clear response to increasing nitrogen
forcing. Its column-averaged $\mathrm{O_3}$ abundance decreased from
approximately $1.1$ times the A1 reference in A0 to $0.52$ times in A3.
This decline is qualitatively consistent with the established coupling
between stratospheric $\mathrm{N_2O}$ photochemistry, $\mathrm{NO_x}$, and
catalytic ozone destruction
\citep{pratherMeasuringNitrousOxideLifetime2015,
grenfellPotentialBiosignaturesSuperEarth2013}. Because $\mathrm{NH_3}$ and
$\mathrm{N_2O}$ were increased simultaneously across the ExoFarm sequence,
the present calculations do not separate their individual contributions to
the ozone response.

Over the same sequence, $\mathrm{CH_4}$ decreased from approximately
$1.2$ to $0.29$ times its A1 value despite an unchanged methane
lower-boundary condition. The methane response therefore emerged as a
secondary change within the photochemical network following the increased
nitrogen forcing.

OH is one of the principal oxidants in atmospheric chemistry and contributes
to the removal of reduced trace gases, including $\mathrm{CH_4}$ and
$\mathrm{NH_3}$. Although its column-averaged abundance changed little
across A0--A3 in either planetary environment, its vertical distribution
differed substantially between them. In the TRAPPIST-1e simulations, OH was
less abundant through much of the lower atmosphere than in the Earth--Sun
case. Because reaction with OH is an important gas-phase loss pathway for
$\mathrm{NH_3}$
\citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}, this vertical structure is
consistent with reduced oxidative removal of ammonia during its transport
away from the surface.

At lower pressures, OH became more abundant while $\mathrm{NH_3}$ remained
elevated over a broad atmospheric region. The vertical behavior therefore
indicates contributions from several processes, including OH oxidation,
direct photolysis, vertical transport, and other reaction pathways. Direct
photolysis and reaction with OH have both been identified as important loss
processes for atmospheric ammonia
\citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}. Reduced photolytic loss
under the TRAPPIST-1 stellar spectrum may also contribute to the enhanced
vertical persistence of $\mathrm{NH_3}$
\citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a,
seguraBiosignaturesEarthLikePlanets2005,
grenfellPotentialBiosignaturesSuperEarth2013}. Quantitatively separating
oxidation, photolysis, transport, and the remaining chemical pathways would
require an explicit reaction-rate or chemical-pathway analysis.

The atmospheric response to ExoFarm forcing therefore depended strongly on
the chemical environment. $\mathrm{N_2O}$ retained an approximately
proportional response to its imposed surface flux, while the abundance and
vertical persistence of $\mathrm{NH_3}$ were more strongly shaped by
photochemical processing. The accompanying changes in $\mathrm{O_3}$,
$\mathrm{CH_4}$, and OH place these nitrogen species within the broader
atmospheric response to the stellar radiation field. The resulting vertical
profiles were subsequently used as inputs for the transmission- and
thermal-emission calculations.
\section{Transmission Spectroscopy of ExoFarm Signatures}
\label{sec:transmission}

TRAPPIST-1e provides a favorable test case for translating the ExoFarm
photochemical perturbation into an observable transmission signal. Its small
ultracool host enhances the relative atmospheric imprint during transit,
making the system particularly attractive for JWST characterization
\citep{gillonTRAPPIST1JWSTCommunity2020}. At the same time, the planet's terrestrial bulk properties
and temperate irradiation have motivated a broad range of atmospheric and
habitability studies
\citep{agolRefiningTransittimingPhotometric2021,turbetReviewPossiblePlanetary2020,linDifferentiatingModernPrebiotic2021}.
These characteristics, together with the extensive use of TRAPPIST-1e in
forward-model and detectability studies of Earth-like atmospheres
\citep{kalteneggerHighresolutionTransmissionSpectra2020,
linKaltenegger2022,lustigYaegerEarthTransitingExoplanet2023,
duquecastanoMachineassistedClassification2025},
make it a natural benchmark for assessing whether the chemical signatures
predicted in \autoref{sec:chemres} remain accessible in transmission.

We propagated the VULCAN pressure--temperature and chemical-abundance profiles
into transmission spectra using POSEIDON
\citep{macdonaldPOSEIDONMultidimensionalAtmospheric2023}.
Following the TRAPPIST-1e atmospheric setup of
\citet{linDifferentiatingModernPrebiotic2021}, we adopted
$T_{\mathrm{eff}}=2559$ K, $\log g_\star=5.21$,
$[\mathrm{Fe/H}]=0.04$, $M_p=0.6356\,M_\oplus$, and
$T_{\mathrm{eq}}=255$ K. The stellar and planetary radii were set to
$R_\star=0.1192\,R_\odot$ and $R_p=0.920\,R_\oplus$, respectively,
following the refined system dimensions of \citet{agol2021}. The same stellar
and planetary configuration was retained across A0--A3.

For the atmospheric structure, we retained the 1 bar modern-Earth-like
pressure--temperature profile calculated by
\citet{linDifferentiatingModernPrebiotic2021} with the coupled
climate--photochemistry model Exo-Prime2. Their TRAPPIST-1e modern-Earth
scenario adopts an enhanced carbon dioxide abundance,
$X_{\mathrm{CO_2}}\simeq0.036$, to maintain a global-mean surface
temperature above the freezing point of water under TRAPPIST-1 irradiation.
As described in \autoref{sec:photo}, vertical transport was represented using
the present-day Earth $K_{zz}$ profile adopted in the VULCAN validation of
\citet{tsaiComparativeStudyAtmospheric2021}, interpolated onto the
TRAPPIST-1e pressure grid.

The spectroscopy calculation treated $\mathrm{N_2}$ as the bulk atmospheric
gas and included $\mathrm{H_2O}$, $\mathrm{CO_2}$, $\mathrm{CH_4}$,
$\mathrm{O_2}$, $\mathrm{O_3}$, $\mathrm{N_2O}$, and $\mathrm{NH_3}$
as molecular absorbers. We used the POSEIDON High-T opacity database in
opacity-sampling mode, which compiles molecular line data from ExoMol,
HITEMP, and HITRAN for the species considered here
\citep{polyanskyExoMolMolecularLine2018,
yurchenkoExoMolMolecularLine2020,
colesExoMolMolecularLine2019,
hargreavesSpectroscopicLineParameters2019,
gordonHITRAN2020MolecularSpectroscopic2022}.
The High-T database was selected because $\mathrm{NH_3}$, one of the target
species in this study, is not included in the corresponding Temperate set.
Rayleigh scattering and the available collision-induced absorption continua
were included through the POSEIDON opacity treatment
\citep{macdonaldPOSEIDONMultidimensionalAtmospheric2023}. No additional cloud or aerosol opacity was included.

For each scenario, POSEIDON calculates the wavelength-dependent transit depth,

\begin{equation}
D(\lambda)
=
\left[
\frac{R_p(\lambda)}{R_\star}
\right]^2 .
\label{eq:transit_depth}
\end{equation}

The native POSEIDON spectra were computed at a constant resolving power of
$R=10{,}000$. The resulting spectra and their molecular contributions are
shown in \autoref{fig:pure}. At the scale of the total transit depth, the four
ExoFarm scenarios remain closely superposed, while the atmospheric response
appears as a much smaller wavelength-dependent structure.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {imgs/trappist1e_pure_a0_molecular_residuals_v2.pdf}
    \caption{
    Transmission spectra and net molecular contributions for the TRAPPIST-1e
    ExoFarm sequence across $0.5$--$20\,\mu\mathrm{m}$. The upper panel shows
    the transmission spectra for A0--A3. A0 is shown in gray, while A1, A2,
    and A3 are shown in cyan, green, and magenta, respectively; the shaded
    regions indicate the spectral difference between each agricultural
    scenario and A0. The three lower panels show the net contributions of
    $\mathrm{N_2O}$, $\mathrm{NH_3}$, and $\mathrm{H_2O}$, respectively,
    obtained as the change in the spectrum when only the selected molecular
    profile is returned to its A0 state while the remaining atmosphere is kept
    fixed. The molecular contributions follow the same A1--A3 color scheme,
    while the darker curves show the corresponding total $A_i-A0$ spectral
    residual.
    }
    \label{fig:pure}
\end{figure*}

To describe where the scenario-dependent chemical changes appear in the
spectrum, we define a molecular signal for each species of interest. This
quantity measures the change in transit depth associated with the variation
of a selected molecular abundance profile relative to A0 while preserving
the remaining atmospheric state of the corresponding scenario. It therefore
provides both the wavelength at which the chemical perturbation is expressed
and its amplitude in transit-depth units, which can later be compared directly
with the instrumental uncertainty.

We first define the total spectral difference relative to the pre-agricultural
atmosphere as

\begin{equation}
\Delta D_{A_j-A0}(\lambda)
=
D_{A_j}(\lambda)-D_{A0}(\lambda).
\label{eq:scenario_residual}
\end{equation}

For an individual molecule $X$ in scenario $A_j$, we then recompute the
spectrum after replacing only the vertical profile of $X$ with its A0
profile. The pressure--temperature structure and all other molecular profiles
remain unchanged. The molecular signal is defined as

\begin{equation}
\Delta_X(A_j,\lambda)
=
D_{\mathrm{full}}(A_j,\lambda)
-
D_{X\rightarrow A0}(A_j,\lambda).
\label{eq:molecular_signal}
\end{equation}

We applied this procedure to $\mathrm{N_2O}$, $\mathrm{NH_3}$, and
$\mathrm{H_2O}$. In this form, $\Delta_X$ quantifies the
wavelength-dependent spectral imprint associated with the change in molecule
$X$ between A0 and $A_j$. Since the surrounding atmospheric composition is
retained, this signal depends on the full atmospheric state and is not
expected to form an exactly additive decomposition of the total
$A_j-A0$ difference.

The nitrogen-bearing molecular signals grow systematically along the
agricultural sequence. In A3, the $\mathrm{N_2O}$ signal reaches
approximately $10.9$, $13.3$, and $17.5$ ppm in the regions near
$2.9$, $4.5$, and $8.6\,\mu\mathrm{m}$, respectively. The strongest
$\mathrm{NH_3}$ signal reaches about $8.3$ ppm near
$10.7\,\mu\mathrm{m}$. The largest local modification of the modeled transit
depth therefore approaches $18$ ppm in the extreme ExoFarm scenario.
The enhanced nitrogen chemistry is expressed across both the near- and
mid-infrared, although the observational weight of these features varies
considerably with wavelength.

We evaluated this observational limitation using PandExo
\citep{batalhaPandExoCommunityTool2017} for JWST NIRSpec PRISM and MIRI LRS.
The adopted wavelength grids cover approximately
$0.6$--$5.3\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ and $5$--$12\,\mu\mathrm{m}$,
respectively. The simulations use $J=11.354$, a total observing baseline of
three transit durations, an 80\% detector saturation limit, and no additional
imposed noise floor. For the present analysis, spectral uncertainties were
treated as independent, with the uncertainty in bin $i$ after
$N_{\rm tr}$ transits scaling as

\begin{equation}
\sigma_i(N_{\rm tr})
=
\frac{\sigma_{i,1}}{\sqrt{N_{\rm tr}}},
\label{eq:noise_scaling}
\end{equation}

where $\sigma_{i,1}$ is the corresponding one-transit PandExo uncertainty.

For the observability analysis, we focus on $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and
$\mathrm{NH_3}$, the two nitrogen-bearing species most directly associated
with the agricultural perturbation. Their molecular signals were evaluated
on the same wavelength grids used for the instrumental uncertainties. Rather
than selecting a single favorable spectral feature, we combined the signal
over the complete observed wavelength range of each instrument. The
one-transit integrated molecular SNR is defined as

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SNR}_{X,k}(1)
=
\left[
\sum_{i\in k}
\left(
\frac{\Delta_{X,i}}
{\sigma_{i,1}}
\right)^2
\right]^{1/2},
\label{eq:integrated_snr}
\end{equation}

where $k$ denotes either the NIRSpec PRISM or MIRI LRS wavelength grid.
This statistic combines the wavelength-dependent information carried by the
molecular signal throughout each instrumental range without requiring
boundaries to be assigned to individual absorption features. Under the
adopted noise scaling,

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SNR}_{X,k}(N_{\rm tr})
=
\sqrt{N_{\rm tr}}\,
\mathrm{SNR}_{X,k}(1).
\label{eq:snr_scaling}
\end{equation}

The resulting molecular sensitivities and their scaling with transit number
are shown in \autoref{fig:snr}.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {imgs/trappist1e_molecular_snr_by_instrument.pdf}
    \caption{
    Integrated molecular SNR for the TRAPPIST-1e ExoFarm sequence.
    Panel (a) shows the SNR obtained by integrating the $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and
    $\mathrm{NH_3}$ molecular signals over the observed wavelength grid of
    each instrument. Circles, squares, and triangles denote A1, A2, and A3,
    respectively, while cyan, green, and violet indicate 1, 10, and
    100 transits. Panel (b) shows the A3 SNR as a function of the number of
    transits. $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ are shown in terracotta and
    violet, respectively; solid curves correspond to NIRSpec PRISM and dashed
    curves to MIRI LRS. Horizontal reference lines mark
    $\mathrm{SNR}=3$ and 5, with the associated transit numbers indicated at
    each intersection.
    }
    \label{fig:snr}
\end{figure*}

Among the combinations considered, $\mathrm{N_2O}$ observed with NIRSpec
PRISM carries the strongest transmission signal. For A3, its integrated SNR
increases from approximately $0.29$ in one transit to $0.93$ after
10 transits and $2.9$ after 100 transits. MIRI retains sensitivity to the
enhanced $\mathrm{N_2O}$ signal, with corresponding A3 values of
approximately $0.09$, $0.29$, and $0.93$. A1 and A2 follow the same
progression at substantially smaller SNR.

The $\mathrm{NH_3}$ signal is considerably less accessible. Even for A3,
its integrated SNR remains well below unity after 100 transits with either
instrument. The extrapolation in \autoref{fig:snr}b provides a useful measure
of the observational scale. Under continued $N_{\rm tr}^{-1/2}$ noise
scaling, the A3 $\mathrm{N_2O}$ signal with NIRSpec reaches
$\mathrm{SNR}=3$ after approximately 105 transits and
$\mathrm{SNR}=5$ after approximately 290 transits. The corresponding MIRI
requirements are of order $10^3$ transits. For $\mathrm{NH_3}$, the formal
requirements exceed $10^4$ transits even for MIRI and extend to several
$10^5$ transits with NIRSpec.

These values leave little observational margin for separating the ExoFarm
scenarios with JWST transmission spectroscopy. The integrated SNR is evaluated
under comparatively favorable assumptions: the wavelength dependence of the
molecular signal is known in advance and its contribution is accumulated
coherently across the instrumental range. Degeneracies among overlapping
absorbers, correlations with other atmospheric parameters, aerosols, and
stellar contamination are not included in this estimate and can further
reduce the information available during atmospheric inference. Even under
these conditions, only the extreme A3 $\mathrm{N_2O}$ case approaches
$\mathrm{SNR}=3$ after 100 NIRSpec transits, while A1, A2, and the
$\mathrm{NH_3}$ signatures remain substantially less accessible.

The transmission analysis therefore characterizes the ExoFarm molecular
signatures under favorable observing assumptions. Whether these signatures
remain distinguishable once the atmospheric composition is inferred jointly,
including molecular and parameter degeneracies, is examined in the retrieval
analysis of \autoref{sec:retrieval}.

[SIGUE MATERIAL VIEJO]


\section{Thermal-Emission Spectroscopy of ExoFarm Signatures}
\label{sec:thermal}

The transmission analysis in \autoref{sec:transmission} shows that the ExoFarm signatures remain challenging for JWST even under the strongest agricultural forcing considered here. We therefore examined their accessibility through direct thermal emission in the mid-infrared. Previous LIFE studies identify nearby temperate terrestrial planets as particularly favorable targets for this observing geometry. Using the reference LIFE configuration, \citet{carrion-gonzalezLargeInterferometerExoplanets2023} obtained a broadband planetary S/N of approximately 170 in 10 h for Proxima Centauri b. At the molecular level, \citet{angerhausenLargeInterferometerExoplanets2024} showed strong $\mathrm{N_2O}$ signatures for an Earth-like planet placed at the actual distance of Proxima Centauri in a one-day LIFE observation and identified nearby systems of this type as ``golden targets'' for mid-infrared characterization.

Motivated by this observational regime, we modeled a controlled Earth-like atmospheric analog irradiated by Proxima Centauri. The atmospheric composition and pressure--temperature structure provide the terrestrial control, while the Proxima system sets the stellar irradiation and observing geometry. Following the Proxima Centauri b system configuration adopted in LIFE X \citep{carrion-gonzalezLargeInterferometerExoplanets2023}, we used a system distance of $1.30$ pc, $T_{\mathrm{eff}}=3050$ K, $R_\star=0.14\,R_\odot$, $R_p=1.23\,R_\oplus$, $M_p=1.59\,M_\oplus$, and $a=0.0485$ AU. The pressure--temperature and $K_{zz}$ profiles were retained from the terrestrial control, while the chemical profiles were recalculated with VULCAN under the Proxima Centauri irradiation field for the A0--A3 surface-forcing sequence introduced in \autoref{sec:photo}.

The thermal-emission calculation followed the atmospheric-species and opacity configuration described for the transmission spectra in \autoref{sec:transmission}. The Proxima-irradiated VULCAN pressure--temperature and abundance profiles were used to compute the directly observed planetary thermal flux at the distance of the system. We assumed a cloud- and aerosol-free atmosphere, neglected thermal scattering, and placed an opaque gray surface at 1 bar with zero surface albedo and unit thermal emissivity. The native planetary emission spectra were computed at a constant resolving power of $R=10{,}000$.

The resulting A0--A3 thermal-emission spectra are shown in \autoref{fig:life_emission}. As in \autoref{sec:transmission}, the molecular contribution of a selected species was isolated by reverting only its vertical abundance profile to the corresponding A0 profile while retaining the remaining atmospheric state. The same counterfactual construction was applied directly to the planetary thermal flux.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {imgs/emission_molecular_residuals_proxima.pdf}
    \caption{
    Thermal-emission spectra and net molecular contributions for the
    Proxima-irradiated Earth-like ExoFarm sequence. The upper panel shows
    the observed planetary thermal flux for A0--A3. A0 is shown in gray,
    while A1, A2, and A3 are shown in cyan, green, and magenta,
    respectively. The horizontal bar marks the
    $4$--$18.5\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ wavelength range adopted for LIFE. The two
    lower panels show the net contributions of $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and
    $\mathrm{NH_3}$, respectively, obtained by returning only the selected
    molecular profile to its A0 state while retaining the remaining
    atmospheric state of each scenario. The molecular contributions follow
    the same A1--A3 color scheme, while the darker curves show the
    corresponding total $A_i-A0$ thermal-flux residual, allowing the
    molecule-specific contribution to be compared with the full spectral
    change.
    }
    \label{fig:life_emission}
\end{figure*}

The total thermal spectra remain closely superposed across most of the wavelength range, while the differential panels reveal the spectral response to increasing agricultural forcing. The $\mathrm{N_2O}$ perturbation extends across several mid-infrared regions, including prominent structure around $7$--$9\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ and a broader contribution toward $17\,\mu\mathrm{m}$. The $\mathrm{NH_3}$ signal is concentrated more strongly around the $10$--$11\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ region. Both molecular contributions grow along the A1--A3 sequence. Their detectability depends on the wavelength-dependent sensitivity of the interferometric observation.

\subsection{ExoFarm Signals with LIFE}
\label{sec:life_signals}

We evaluated the observational sensitivity with LIFEsim
\citep{dannertLargeInterferometerExoplanets2022}, using the LIFE reference configuration adopted in LIFE X \citep{carrion-gonzalezLargeInterferometerExoplanets2023}. The simulated interferometer contains four 2 m collectors, covers $4$--$18.5\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ at a spectral resolution of $R=20$, and produces 31 spectral channels with a nominal throughput of 5\%. The noise calculation includes photon noise from stellar leakage, local zodiacal emission, and an exozodiacal component of 3 zodis. Channel uncertainties were treated independently, corresponding to the photon-noise-limited LIFE regime considered here. Instrumental stability terms and spectral covariance were not included.

The molecular sensitivity was evaluated over the complete LIFE wavelength grid using the integrated molecular S/N introduced in \autoref{eq:integrated_snr}. For the LIFE calculation, the transmission-depth residual is replaced by the corresponding thermal-flux residual and the spectral uncertainties are those generated by LIFEsim. Under the adopted photon-noise regime, the uncertainty scales as $t^{-1/2}$ and the integrated molecular S/N consequently scales as $\sqrt{t}$.

The resulting sensitivities are shown in \autoref{fig:life_snr}. The A3 scenario produces the strongest molecular signals. For $\mathrm{N_2O}$, the integrated S/N increases from approximately $1.89$ at 1 h to $5.97$ at 10 h and $18.86$ at 100 h. The corresponding $\mathrm{NH_3}$ values are approximately $0.49$, $1.54$, and $4.86$. The A1 enhancement remains considerably weaker, reaching integrated S/N values of approximately $1.92$ for $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $0.37$ for $\mathrm{NH_3}$ after 100 h.

The photon-noise scaling provides an approximate exposure timescale for the A3 molecular templates. The $\mathrm{N_2O}$ signal reaches integrated S/N values of 3 and 5 after approximately 3 and 7 h, respectively. The corresponding times for $\mathrm{NH_3}$ are approximately 38 and 106 h. These values quantify sensitivity to a specified molecular template across the LIFE wavelength range. Atmospheric inference additionally depends on degeneracies among molecular abundances, the thermal structure, and the planetary radius.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
    {imgs/life_classic_snr_integrated_proxima.pdf}
    \caption{
    Integrated LIFE sensitivity to the ExoFarm molecular signals.
    Panel (a) compares the $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ molecular
    templates for A1--A3 at different integration times. Marker shape
    identifies the ExoFarm scenario and color identifies the exposure time.
    The auxiliary $1000$ h point extends the photon-noise scaling beyond the
    focal 1, 10, and 100 h retrieval sequence. Panel (b) shows the projected
    A3 integrated S/N as a function of exposure time for $\mathrm{N_2O}$
    and $\mathrm{NH_3}$. Horizontal lines mark S/N values of 3 and 5, and
    the corresponding crossing times are indicated for each molecule.
    }
    \label{fig:life_snr}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Atmospheric Retrieval with LIFE}
\label{sec:life_retrieval}

The molecular-template S/N quantifies the sensitivity to a specified wavelength-dependent signal. Atmospheric retrieval provides a stronger test by allowing the molecular composition, pressure--temperature structure, and planetary radius to vary simultaneously. Degeneracies among these quantities represent the principal challenge for determining whether the enhanced $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ abundances remain identifiable from the observed thermal spectrum.

Synthetic LIFE datasets were generated for A1 and A3 at 1, 10, and 100 h and analyzed with POSEIDON and MultiNest. The retrieval forward model preserved the same direct-emission geometry, molecular opacity set, cloud-free assumption, and opaque 1 bar surface used to generate the synthetic spectra. Its atmospheric state was parameterized independently through the molecular abundances, the pressure--temperature profile, and the planetary radius.

Molecular composition was retrieved using an isochemical profile, in which each species is represented by a single volume mixing ratio that remains constant with pressure. This parametrization allows the data to constrain an effective atmospheric abundance for each molecule while keeping the dimensionality of the retrieval compatible with the 31 LIFE spectral channels. The synthetic spectra themselves retain the pressure-dependent VULCAN profiles, so the retrieval tests how effectively a single representative abundance can reproduce the spectral information carried by the vertically resolved chemistry.

The pressure--temperature structure was described with the six-parameter profile of \citet{madhusudhanTemperatureAbundanceRetrieval2009}. This parametrization divides the atmosphere into three analytic pressure regions and uses $a_1$ and $a_2$ to control the thermal gradients, $P_1$, $P_2$, and $P_3$ to locate transitions between the regions and permit both inverted and non-inverted structures, and $T_{\mathrm{ref}}$ to set the overall temperature scale. Its flexibility allows a broad family of thermal structures to be explored with relatively few parameters, which is advantageous for the low-resolution LIFE spectra considered here.

The adopted parameter ranges were chosen as broad operational priors for a temperate rocky atmosphere. The interval $T_{\mathrm{ref}}=150$--$400$ K encompasses thermal states around the Earth-like input profile and the temperate Proxima irradiation regime. Positive values $a_1,a_2\in[0.02,2]$ allow shallow through steep thermal gradients while avoiding the singular zero scale of the analytic parametrization. The transition-pressure priors allow the thermal structure to vary over several atmospheric scale heights: $\log_{10}(P_1/\mathrm{bar})$ and $\log_{10}(P_2/\mathrm{bar})$ span $[-6,1]$, while $\log_{10}(P_3/\mathrm{bar})$ spans $[-2,1]$. The portions of these pressure priors deeper than the fixed 1 bar surface do not contribute directly to the emergent spectrum and are retained to reproduce the parameter space used in the executed retrieval campaign. The functional form follows \citet{madhusudhanTemperatureAbundanceRetrieval2009}, while the numerical bounds are choices of the present analysis.

The planetary surface was fixed at $P_{\mathrm{surf}}=1$ bar, matching the opaque lower boundary used to generate the synthetic spectra. Fixing this pressure prevents the lower boundary from introducing an additional pressure--radius--temperature degeneracy. The reference radius remained free because the absolute thermal flux depends on the emitting area of the planet. Molecular abundances were assigned broad logarithmic priors so that weak species could approach negligible concentrations while the enhanced ExoFarm abundances remained well within the explored parameter space. The complete forward-model configuration and priors are summarized in \autoref{tab:life_retrieval_setup}.

\begin{table*}[t!]
\centering
\caption{
Forward-model parameterization and priors adopted for the LIFE retrievals.
The chemistry is isochemical, with one vertically constant volume mixing ratio
for each retrieved species. Pressure parameters are expressed in
$\log_{10}(\mathrm{bar})$.
}
\label{tab:life_retrieval_setup}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Parameter & Prior / adopted value & Role in the retrieval \\
\hline

$a_1$, $a_2$
    & $\mathcal{U}(0.02,\,2)$
    & Thermal-gradient scales in the Madhusudhan--Seager profile \\

$\log P_1$, $\log P_2$
    & $\mathcal{U}(-6,\,1)$
    & Upper and intermediate P--T transition pressures \\

$\log P_3$
    & $\mathcal{U}(-2,\,1)$
    & Deep P--T transition pressure \\

$T_{\mathrm{ref}}$
    & $\mathcal{U}(150,\,400)$ K
    & Overall temperature scale \\

$R_{p,\mathrm{ref}}$
    & $\mathcal{U}(0.8,\,1.3)\times R_{p,\mathrm{nom}}$
    & Planetary reference radius \\

$\log_{10}(\mathrm{VMR})$
    & $\mathcal{U}(-12,\,-1)$
    & Isochemical $\mathrm{H_2O}$, $\mathrm{CO_2}$, $\mathrm{CH_4}$,
      $\mathrm{O_3}$, $\mathrm{N_2O}$, and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ \\

$\log_{10}(\mathrm{VMR}_{\mathrm{O_2}})$
    & $\mathcal{U}(-6,\,\log_{10}0.5)$
    & Isochemical $\mathrm{O_2}$ abundance \\
$N_{\mathrm{live}}$
    & $800$
    & MultiNest live points \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The 1, 10, and 100 h datasets use independent noise realizations. The comparison among exposure times therefore reflects the increase in information content together with the particular noise realization associated with each dataset.

The synthetic spectra contain pressure-dependent VULCAN abundance profiles, whereas the retrieval returns one isochemical abundance for each species. To place both descriptions on a common scale, we defined the effective A0 abundance as the pressure-weighted mean

\begin{equation}
\overline{X}_{A0,\mathrm{eff}}
=
\frac{
\displaystyle
\int_{p_{\mathrm{top}}}^{p_{\mathrm{bot}}}
X_{A0}(p)\,dp
}{
p_{\mathrm{bot}}-p_{\mathrm{top}}
}.
\label{eq:a0_effective}
\end{equation}

Under hydrostatic conditions, this weighting approximates a column-mass average and provides a single reference abundance that can be compared directly with the vertically constant retrieval posterior.

For the visualization in \autoref{fig:life_retrievals}, the displacement of each posterior from this reference is summarized by

\begin{equation}
z^{\mathrm{desc}}_{s|A0}
=
\frac{
q_{50,s}
-
\log_{10}\left(\overline{X}_{A0,\mathrm{eff}}\right)
}{
\sigma_{68,s}
},
\qquad
\sigma_{68,s}
=
\frac{q_{84,s}-q_{16,s}}{2}.
\label{eq:zdesc}
\end{equation}

The quantity $z^{\mathrm{desc}}$ expresses the displacement of the posterior median from the effective A0 abundance in units of the central posterior half-width. For approximately Gaussian and symmetric posteriors, it can be interpreted as an approximate sigma-equivalent separation from the A0 reference. We use it here as a descriptive measure of how strongly an inferred ExoFarm abundance departs from the pre-agricultural atmospheric state.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]    {imgs/retrieval_A1_A3_1_10_100h_official_six_species_PT_proxima.pdf}
    \caption{
    LIFE atmospheric retrievals for the A1 and A3 Proxima-irradiated
    Earth-like cases at 1, 10, and 100 h. Rows correspond to integration
    time. The first six columns show the marginalized abundance posteriors
    for $\mathrm{H_2O}$, $\mathrm{CO_2}$, $\mathrm{CH_4}$,
    $\mathrm{O_3}$, $\mathrm{N_2O}$, and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ together with
    their corresponding VULCAN vertical profiles. The final column shows
    the retrieved pressure--temperature structures. A1 is shown in green
    and A3 in magenta. Vertical reference lines indicate the effective A0
    abundances. Each molecular panel reports the posterior median and the
    descriptive displacement $z^{\mathrm{desc}}_{s|A0}$ defined in
    \autoref{eq:zdesc}. Posterior densities are normalized independently
    within each panel. $\mathrm{O_2}$ is included in the retrieval but
    omitted from the atlas for compactness.
    }
    \label{fig:life_retrievals}
\end{figure}

At 1 h, neither nitrogen-bearing species is strongly constrained. The A3 molecular templates remain below the nominal S/N = 3 level, with integrated S/N values of $1.89$ for $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $0.49$ for $\mathrm{NH_3}$, and their retrieved abundances remain close to the effective A0 reference, with $z^{\mathrm{desc}}=-0.88$ and $+0.14$, respectively. The remaining retrieved species likewise show no comparably strong scenario-dependent displacement from their A0 references and primarily provide atmospheric context for the nitrogen-bearing species.

At 10 h, the behavior of $\mathrm{N_2O}$ changes markedly in the extreme A3 case. Its molecular template reaches an integrated S/N of $5.97$, while the retrieved abundance concentrates around $\log_{10}(\mathrm{VMR})=-6.06$ and is displaced from the effective A0 abundance by $z^{\mathrm{desc}}=5.59$. The A3 $\mathrm{N_2O}$ enhancement is therefore clearly separated from the pre-agricultural reference within the adopted retrieval model. The corresponding $\mathrm{NH_3}$ posterior remains much less informative, with $z^{\mathrm{desc}}=0.85$, indicating that 10 h is insufficient to separate its A3 abundance from A0.

The distinction becomes stronger at 100 h. For A3, $\mathrm{N_2O}$ reaches $z^{\mathrm{desc}}=13.24$, while $\mathrm{NH_3}$ reaches $z^{\mathrm{desc}}=5.63$ and an integrated molecular S/N of $4.86$. The longer exposure therefore provides the first case in this sequence where the extreme ExoFarm enhancement is clearly separated from A0 for both nitrogen-bearing species. In contrast, the A1 cases remain close to the A0 reference even at 100 h, with $z^{\mathrm{desc}}=-0.99$ for $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $-0.77$ for $\mathrm{NH_3}$. The A1 $\mathrm{NH_3}$ template also remains extremely weak, with an integrated S/N of only $0.37$. Consequently, the present-day-like agricultural perturbation is not distinguishable from A0 in these observations, while the extreme A3 scenario produces a characteristic joint displacement in $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and, at sufficiently long exposure, $\mathrm{NH_3}$.

\textbf{[PENDIENTE: añadir un párrafo específico sobre detección molecular. Aquí se debe distinguir entre la detectabilidad de la presencia de una molécula y la capacidad de diferenciar su abundancia de la referencia A0. Por ahora se mantiene $z^{\mathrm{desc}}$ como medida de separación respecto de A0. Más adelante se evaluará si conviene complementar esta métrica usando directamente la probabilidad posterior en la cola respecto de A0, $p_{\mathrm{tail}}$, y su correspondiente separación gaussiana equivalente.]}

This distinction is particularly important for the interpretation of $\mathrm{N_2O}$. Large biological $\mathrm{N_2O}$ fluxes can arise when denitrification terminates inefficiently at $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and the final reduction to $\mathrm{N_2}$ becomes limited \citep{schwietermanEvaluatingPlausibleRange2022}. Such pathways can sustain $\mathrm{N_2O}$ fluxes far above the modern terrestrial value independently of an agricultural technosphere. Atmospheric $\mathrm{NH_3}$ responds to a different source--sink balance. Dissolution, conversion to $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$, deposition, and uptake by aqueous and surface reservoirs can remove $\mathrm{NH_3}$ efficiently, while atmospheric accumulation becomes much more favorable as these sinks approach saturation \citep{huangAssessmentAmmoniaBiosignature2022a}.

The joint recovery of enhanced $\mathrm{N_2O}$ and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ consequently provides greater attribution power than $\mathrm{N_2O}$ alone. Within the ExoFarm framework, $\mathrm{N_2O}$ provides the most accessible initial tracer of the nitrogen perturbation. The recovery of $\mathrm{NH_3}$ at longer integration times adds an independent constraint on the sustained input of reduced nitrogen and its competition with efficient surface sinks. Simultaneous recovery of the surrounding atmospheric composition further constrains the chemical environment in which both species coexist. Together, these measurements provide a stronger atmospheric indication of sustained agricultural forcing.

At 100 h, the increased precision also exposes limitations of the retrieval parameterization, particularly the use of vertically constant abundances to represent the pressure-dependent VULCAN chemistry; the corresponding posterior separations should therefore be interpreted within this model assumption.

\bigskip

Overall, the thermal-emission analysis places the extreme ExoFarm scenario in a substantially more favorable observational regime than the JWST transmission case in \autoref{sec:transmission}. In A3, $\mathrm{N_2O}$ reaches informative LIFE sensitivities on timescales of hours and becomes clearly separated from the A0 atmospheric state in the retrieval by 10 h. Longer integrations allow the $\mathrm{NH_3}$ enhancement to become similarly distinguishable while also constraining the broader atmospheric context, substantially increasing the diagnostic power of the inferred nitrogen perturbation. For the nearest temperate systems, the observational challenge therefore progresses from molecular sensitivity toward the separation of agricultural atmospheric states from plausible natural references and the control of retrieval degeneracies.


