To simulate the atmospheric response to varying nitrogen emission regimes, we employ the 1D chemical kinetics code VULCAN \citep{tsaiVULCANOpensourceValidated2017}. We use the standard \texttt{NCHO\_earth\_photo\_network}, which comprises 47 chemical species and solves a total of 715 reactions. This network is specifically optimized for oxidizing $N_2-O_2$ dominated atmospheres and includes 528 two-body reactions, 88 three-body reactions, and 99 photodissociation reactions. For all simulations, we model a planet with Earth-like physical parameters using the default U.S. Standard Atmosphere 1976 thermal profile (\texttt{atm\_Earth\_standard}). Vertical transport is handled via VULCAN's standard parameterized diffusion profile for terrestrial atmospheres, which captures efficient convective mixing in the troposphere and its subsequent decay into the stratosphere.

The impact of the spectral energy distribution (SED) on photochemistry is isolated by comparing two extreme stellar environments. The first, a G2V control case, uses the standard solar spectrum provided in VULCAN to represent a high Near-UV (NUV) flux environment. The second, an M8V case, utilizes observational data from the Mega-MUSCLES program \citep{wilsonMegaMUSCLESSpectralEnergy2021}, integrating Hubble UV observations and X-ray models. These fluxes are geometrically scaled to match the bolometric irradiation at the orbit of TRAPPIST-1e. While the G2V spectrum is NUV-rich, the M8V environment is characterized by significantly lower NUV emission but heightened Lyman-$\alpha$ and XUV activity.

A key distinction of this work compared to previous studies \citep[e.g.,][]{haqq-misraDisruptionPlanetaryNitrogen2022} is the use of surface flux boundary conditions instead of fixed atmospheric abundances. This physically dynamic approach allows the atmosphere to evolve toward a photochemical steady state governed by the destruction efficiency of each star's SED. We define four ``ExoFarm'' emission scenarios by scaling present-day terrestrial nitrogen fluxes \citep{tianComprehensiveQuantificationGlobal2020}. Scenario A1 represents modern Earth, while A0 establishes a purely biological baseline by removing anthropogenic components. Scenarios A2 and A3 represent intensified agricultural activity, applying multiplicative scaling factors ($10\times$ and $100\times$, respectively) consistent with planetary carrying capacity limits \citep{battyeNitrogenNextCarbon2017,cohenPopulationGrowthEarths1995}. These four regimes, summarized in Table \ref{tab:scenarios}, are integrated into a crossed factorial design to evaluate their interaction with the two radiative environments.


