Because the spectral information of a given molecule is distributed across multiple bands and can be confused with other absorbers, forward spectra alone are insufficient to establish practical detectability. The retrieval stage therefore adopts an isothermal, iso-compositional atmospheric setup without stellar contamination as a first working approximation and explores the abundances of all relevant molecules simultaneously. Its scientific role is to evaluate not only whether a feature is present, but whether observing campaigns can distinguish among agricultural scenarios once spectral degeneracies are explicitly allowed. A compact visual summary of this step is given in \autoref{fig:ret}.

Within the current working interpretation, $NH_3$ remains difficult to retrieve robustly with JWST even under extreme scenarios, whereas $N_2O$ is expected to be the more accessible discriminant in combined near-infrared instrument configurations and at very large transit counts. These statements should still be treated as provisional, but the retrieval section closes the gap between chemically amplified atmospheres and what an actual observing campaign could plausibly infer under a deliberately simplified inverse model.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{imgs/trappist_retrieved_zooms_A0_A3_v2.pdf}
    \caption{Retrieval summary for the TRAPPIST-1e ExoFarm extreme-contrast test between A0 and A3. The figure compares the true model, the noisy synthetic observations, and the retrieved spectra in selected molecular windows, illustrating how the inferred signal quality changes with transit count. The panels emphasize the relative recoverability of the strongest $N_2O$ bands and the weaker, more demanding $NH_3$ feature.}
    \label{fig:ret}
\end{figure*}

